  "captions": [
   {
    "t": 0,
    "text": "1 July 1916, 6 am. For a week some 1,500 British guns have fired over a million and a half shells at the German lines. The last hour is the heaviest."
   },
   {
    "t": 1.33,
    "text": "7.20 am. A huge mine explodes under Hawthorn Ridge, near Beaumont-Hamel. It warns the Germans that the attack is coming."
   },
   {
    "t": 1.47,
    "text": "7.28 am. More mines go up, the biggest at La Boisselle. It leaves the Lochnagar crater, still there today."
   },
   {
    "t": 1.5,
    "text": "7.30 am. Whistles blow. Along 25 kilometres British infantry climb out and cross no man's land in long lines, many at a walk."
   },
   {
    "t": 2,
    "text": "In the north the German machine gunners come up from deep dugouts in time. At Serre, Beaumont-Hamel and Ovillers whole battalions are cut down in no man's land."
   },
   {
    "t": 3.25,
    "text": "9.15 am. The Newfoundland Regiment attacks at Beaumont-Hamel from the reserve trenches. Of about 800 men, only 68 answer the roll call next day."
   },
   {
    "t": 4,
    "text": "At Thiepval the 36th (Ulster) Division storms the Schwaben Redoubt and pushes on towards the German second line. With no one beside it, it is shelled from three sides."
   },
   {
    "t": 4.5,
    "text": "In the south it goes better. Next to the French, the 18th and 30th Divisions take Montauban by late morning. Mametz falls in the afternoon."
   },
   {
    "t": 6,
    "text": "The French, with far more heavy guns per kilometre, take all their objectives on both banks of the Somme."
   },
   {
    "t": 9,
    "text": "At Gommecourt, a diversion, the 56th Division gets into the German lines but is driven out by evening."
   },
   {
    "t": 15,
    "text": "After dark the Ulstermen, out of bombs and ammunition, are forced back from the Schwaben Redoubt."
   },
   {
    "t": 17,
    "text": "By midnight the British have 57,470 casualties, 19,240 of them dead. The battle goes on for four and a half months."
   }
  ]
